## sess119 — ruling items 3 and 8 landed; the bypass audit found three more unwindowed clears

**Build: VERSION still `0.11.441` (not revved — the landing is incomplete), srcversion `AC44AC4FAF6EF5DA0C24299` → `A78D555EAA0734BC6BBFD6E`. Builds clean, zero warnings. Still STOP-SHIP: fleet stays on 0.11.440.**

I continued D-SAMENODE-WAITER-CANCEL-COLLISION (ledger #16) rather than starting at #1, because the tree is mid-landing under the sess118 stop-ship ruling and leaving a half-wired clear-window mechanism in `dlm_caw.c` to go work another defect is strictly worse than finishing it.

**What landed — ruling items 3 and 8.** A new `caw_may_have_written(rc)` predicate encodes the one thing a destructive clear has to get right: `-EAGAIN` is the *only* definite no-change outcome of a CAW, because that is the target reporting a miscompare. `rc == 0` definitely wrote; everything else — I/O error after retries, transport failure, `-ESHUTDOWN` — may have reached the target and completed. All three clear sites now close their window as committed on the ambiguous class. `force_release_self` needed more than a predicate swap: it issues an independent CAS per slot across up to `CLAIMRACE_SCAN_MAX` slots, so the clear-sequence answer is the OR across every sub-operation, which its `cleared` return value could not carry.

**A real inversion inside `caw_slot` itself.** With `caw_gen_verify=1`, a failed verify-read retried the CAS *after the CAW had already succeeded*. The compare image is stale by then, so the retry miscompares and the function returns `-EAGAIN` — "definitely nothing changed" reported for a write that definitely did change the slot. It is off by default, but it is precisely the inversion item 3 exists to prevent.

**The audit is the more consequential result.** Item (i) requires that no destructive path bypass begin/end. I classified all 30-plus `&= ~ctx->node_bit` sites. Three strip this node's authority with no window at all: both DIVERG arms in `mxfs_dlm_caw_lock`, and the DOWNGRADE arm of `mxfs_dlm_caw_convert` — the last one clears the *higher* mode bit, so a local thread holding `tenure[old_mode]` silently loses its on-disk authority. The rest clear for stated reasons: the grant paths write the slot (so a clear CAS miscompares) and clear only a subsumed lower mode in the same CAS; `yield_to` and `open_holders` are not authority bits; `release_all` is the unmount terminal state the ruling exempts.

Blocker 5's design is settled but unwritten, and it turns out to be nearly free: making `lreq_clr_begin` find-first removes the allocator from the path entirely for any resource this node holds a grant on, since `tenure[]` keeps the entry alive. That kills the "proceed under the legacy mark alone" ENOMEM fallback in unlock without the liveness cost the current comment argues for.

Blockers 1, 2, 5 and 7 remain open.

**Criteria: NO.** Not production ready — 29 open defects, 17 critical, and the DLM core is mid-landing under an active stop-ship ruling. The marker stays unwritten.

